# The platform's dependencies: the SOURCE of platform/requirements.txt, which
# is generated from this file with the sha256 of every distribution file and
# installed in hash-checking mode (Makefile web_sandbox, CI, docs/DEVELOPING.md
# 5.2). Edit here, then regenerate, in the dev container:
#
#     uv pip compile --universal --generate-hashes \
#         -c sandboxd/requirements.in \
#         platform/requirements.in -o platform/requirements.txt
#
# The constraint keeps the packages shared with sandboxd at sandboxd's
# versions, since CI installs both files into one interpreter;
# sandboxd/tests/test_requirements.py checks it, offline.
#
# Password hashing for the credentials this platform issues itself (DRAFT-061
# decision 3). argon2id, and the parameters are NOT left to the library's
# default: `config.Settings` derives them from `RFC_9106_LOW_MEMORY` -- 64 MiB,
# t=3, p=4 -- and caps concurrent hashes, because each one holds its memory cost
# for the whole hash and a burst of logins allocates all of them at once. That
# is a memory bound a rate limiter cannot enforce; see `rate_limit.count` in
# migration 0009.
#
# The module is `argon2`; `argon2-cffi` is only the distribution name.
# `argon2-cffi-bindings` is pinned beside it deliberately rather than floated as
# a transitive: it carries the compiled Argon2 reference implementation, so it
# is the one to look at on a rebuild that behaves differently.
argon2-cffi==25.1.0
argon2-cffi-bindings==26.1.0
boto3==1.43.78
botocore==1.43.78
fastapi==0.141.1
httpx==0.28.1
# The UI (#38). Server-rendered, so the escaping rule is structural:
# autoescape is on by default and the dangerous thing becomes an explicit
# `| safe` a reviewer can grep for. htmx is vendored, not fetched -- a strict
# CSP blocks external hosts, and a malware-analysis platform is the last place
# to introduce an npm dependency tree.
jinja2==3.1.6
pyjwt[crypto]==2.10.1
# TOTP for the second factor (DRAFT-061 decision 3). Pure Python and offline:
# no provider, no per-message cost, and nothing outbound on the login path.
# Chosen over SMS-to-Telegram even though signup already proves a Telegram
# account, because a messaging channel is a delivery mechanism someone else can
# take over -- the Telegram link is a signup gate, not an authentication factor.
pyotp==2.10.0
psycopg==3.3.4
psycopg-binary==3.3.4
psycopg-pool==3.3.1
pydantic==2.13.4
pydantic_core==2.46.4
pytest==9.1.1
# Browser-level tests for the UI (#38). These assert what a source-reading
# test cannot: that a hostile filename does not EXECUTE, judged by an engine
# that parses HTML the way an attacker's target does. Needs a browser:
#   playwright install chromium
pytest-playwright==0.9.0
python-magic==0.4.27
starlette==1.6.0
# Artifact downloads (packaging.py): a streaming ZIP writer -- local headers,
# data descriptors, Zip64, the central directory -- with AES-256 (WinZip
# AE-2), replacing a hand-written ZipCrypto writer on 2026-11-14 (operator).
# AES through pycryptodome, in C, where the ZipCrypto loop was per byte in
# Python. Pinned below its newest (4.4.0, 2026-11-07): a release two weeks
# old has had the least scrutiny.
#
# **Upgrade pycryptodome deliberately.** 4.x warns that stream-zip's
# `PBKDF2(password, salt, n, 1000)` leans on defaults (HMAC-SHA1) that "will
# be removed". AE-2 specifies exactly that derivation, so the warning is not a
# weakness to fix here -- but the release that removes the default breaks every
# download until stream-zip passes SHA1 itself. tests/test_packaging.py
# round-trips through 7-Zip and would fail first.
stream-zip==0.0.84
pycryptodome==4.2.0
# Tests only: reads the AES archives back. The standard library's zipfile
# reads ZipCrypto and not AES.
stream-unzip==0.0.101
uvicorn==0.52.4
# uvicorn does NOT implement the WebSocket protocol itself. Without one of
# these it does not handle an upgrade at all: the request is treated as a plain
# HTTP GET, no HTTP route matches the socket path, and the client gets a 404
# from uvicorn with the route perfectly well registered (DRAFT-056, F6).
#
# That failure is invisible to the test suite, because TestClient speaks ASGI
# directly and never goes through uvicorn's protocol layer -- so a WebSocket
# test can pass while the deployed server cannot serve one.
#
# 16.1.1 rather than 17: sandboxd uses the same library on Dom0, whose Python
# is older than this container's, and 17 has no distribution for it. Pinning
# what the DEVELOPMENT container happened to have made an install that could
# not succeed on the machine it was for. One version both hosts can take is
# worth more than the newest one here.
websockets==16.1.1
